{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8d7cc4ed624560c8","type":"tool_result","content":"150\t25. Implementation with `konflux-onboarding` in dependency chain → add \"build pipeline green\" AC\n151\t\n152\t## Step 5.5: Detect Tiered Delivery\n153\t\n154\tWhen an external dependency has uncertain timing:\n155\t\n156\t1. Can the strategy deliver partial value without it?\n157\t2. **YES** → Split into:\n158\t   - **Tier 1**: Independent work (user-facing features OR risk-reduction spikes/validations)\n159\t   - **Tier 2**: Dependency-gated work (blocked by upstream epic)\n160\t3. **NO** → Linear dependencies; external dependency blocks all downstream\n161\t\n162\t## Step 6: Classify Epics\n163\t\n164\tFor each epic, determine:\n165\t\n166\t### Type (mandatory)\n167\t- **Implementation**: Produce an artifact (code, config, docs, manifests, pipelines, RFCs, upstream PRs)\n168\t- **Investigation**: Resolve uncertainty (answer a question or make a decision that other epics depend on)\n169\t\n170\t### Implementation Type (optional routing label)\n171\t\n172\t| Label | When to apply |\n173\t|-------|--------------|\n174\t| `docs-authoring` | Red Hat docs content process — specialized tooling |\n175\t| `konflux-onboarding` | Build pipeline onboarding — known-recipe |\n176\t| `license-validation` | License scanning for transitive dependencies |\n177\t| `repo-onboarding` | Midstream repo fork creation under opendatahub-io |\n178\t| _(absent/null)_ | General-purpose — read target repo, figure it out |\n179\t\n180\t### AI Implementability Signals\n181\t\n182\tScore signals so the pipeline can compute an AI-implementability classification deterministically. **Do not compute the total score or classification yourself.** Use the signal set that matches the epic `type` — Implementation and Investigation epics measure different things.\n183\t\n184\t#### Implementation epics → `ai_signals` (9 signals)\n185\t\n186\tEvaluate each as +1 (favorable), 0 (neutral/N/A), or -1 (unfavorable). Write the values into `ai_signals`.\n187\t\n188\t| # | Signal | Frontmatter key | +1 Condition | -1 Condition |\n189\t|---|--------|----------------|-------------|-------------|\n190\t| 1 | Change specificity | `change_specificity` | Exact file paths, API endpoints, field names known | Vague scope (\"improve X\") |\n191\t| 2 | Pattern precedent | `pattern_precedent` | Similar changes exist in same codebase | No precedent in codebase |\n192\t| 3 | Adapter/plugin pattern | `adapter_pattern` | Follows existing reference implementation | N/A (0 if absent) |\n193\t| 4 | Existing foundation | `existing_foundation` | Extending existing code/feature | Greenfield, no foundation |\n194\t| 5 | Open questions | `open_questions` | All design decisions resolved for this epic | Open questions that would change implementation approach |\n195\t| 6 | External dependency | `external_dependency` | None | Upstream contribution or vendor coordination needed |\n196\t| 7 | Human process gates | `human_process_gates` | None | Requires human approval step |\n197\t| 8 | Repo access | `repo_access` | AI can clone and modify target repo | Repo inaccessible or special access required |\n198\t| 9 | Architecture claims | `architecture_claims` | Epic's scope or the strategy's premise about the component (surfaces, repos, deployment model, maturity state such as Tech Preview/GA) is corroborated by an architecture-context file — name that file in the rationale | Strategy asserts architecture that the context files contradict or cannot back |\n199\t\n200\t**Scoring `architecture_claims` (signal 9):** a strategy makes architecture claims whenever it states what a component IS — its maturity state (\"shipped as Tech Preview\", \"generally available\"), its CRD/API/repo surfaces, its deployment model — even when it never uses the word \"claim\". A strategy premise that drives the decomposition (e.g. a maturity gap motivating a hardening program) is an architecture claim. Check `.context/architecture-context/` for the epic's components: score +1 when a context file corroborates the premise or grounds the epic's scope, and cite that file in the rationale; score -1 when the strategy asserts something the context contradicts; score 0 only when no context file covers any component the epic touches. \"The strategy makes no specific architecture claims\" is not a valid 0 rationale when context files exist for the components in scope.\n201\t\n202\t#### Investigation epics → `investigation_signals` (5 signals)\n203\t\n204\t**Do not use the 9 Implementation signals for Investigation epics.** Those penalize the *defining* traits of an investigation (open questions, no prior foundation, external dependency), so they mis-route nearly every investigation to Low — even ones the AI resolves easily. Instead score these five, which predict whether the AI investigation skill can resolve the unknowns (→ assign to the skill) or a person must (→ assign to a person). Write the values into `investigation_signals`.\n205\t\n206\t| # | Signal | Frontmatter key | Range | How to score |\n207\t|---|--------|-----------------|-------|--------------|\n208\t| 1 | Question specificity | `question_specificity` | -1 / 0 / +1 | +1 concrete, enumerable questions with a defined pass/fail criterion; -1 vague or criterion undefined (a human must frame it first) |\n209\t| 2 | Source accessibility | `source_accessibility` | 0 / +1 | +1 a readable artifact that **contains or determines the answer** exists (open repo, docs, public record). 0 when the only readable material does **not** hold the answer — e.g. a decision/PR still under review (the answer doesn't exist yet) |\n210\t| 3 | Local runnability | `local_runnability` | 0 / +1 | +1 the components can be exercised as a downloaded **binary or pip/npm library** locally — including backing a datastore with an embedded/bundled server (e.g. `pgserver` for PostgreSQL). 0 only if it genuinely can't run without a container/cluster |\n211\t| 4 | Cluster/hardware dependence | `cluster_hardware_dependence` | -2 / -1 / 0 | 0 none. -1 some *peripheral* questions need a live cluster / GPU / prod-scale. -2 a *gating* question does. **What an operator/controller _generates_** (rendered CR, template, NetworkPolicy, RBAC in its source repo) is readable source — score it under `source_accessibility`, **not** here; only the *as-deployed runtime state* of a live cluster counts as a dependence |\n212\t| 5 | Human judgment required | `human_judgment_required` | -2 / -1 / 0 | 0 none. -1 a *peripheral* question needs a stakeholder/product decision or external-party input. -2 the *gating* question is itself a human/governance decision or external coordination the AI cannot make (e.g. \"is this ADR approved?\") |\n213\t\n214\tThe pipeline routes: **High** → assign to the investigation skill; **Medium** → hybrid (skill resolves the desk/local parts and hands a spec to a human for the rest); **Low** → assign to a person.\n215\t\n216\tWrite the signal rationale to a separate file `artifacts/epic-tasks/{ID}-ENNN-ai-signals.md` (one per epic), for whichever signal set you used. Format as a markdown table with Signal, Value, and Rationale columns. Do not write the signals table into the epic body.\n217\t\n218\t## Step 6.5: Health Warnings\n219\t\n220\t### Non-blocking warnings (decomposition proceeds, human verifies later)\n221\t- Priority inversions (P0/P1/P2 inconsistent with technical complexity/reliability)\n222\t- Scope traps: ACs implying infrastructure that doesn't exist, contradictions between out-of-scope items and NFRs, storage beyond design intent\n223\t- **Epic-size overages — always record a re-split trigger.** When the strategy's effort estimate or your scope arithmetic puts an epic above ~2 weeks (one sprint) of work and you keep it as ONE logical change — a single mechanical pass over one surface, one audit, where a split would duplicate the audit or fragment the change — do not leave the overage as a bare note. Record a **re-split trigger**: the concrete planning condition under which the epic should be re-split (e.g. \"re-split if the call-site audit exceeds the estimated volume\", \"split the logistics work out if the campaign outgrows a sprint of effort\"). A justified overage with a recorded trigger is a managed trade-off; a bare \"above guideline, noted for planning awareness only\" note is an unresolved defect that every later review will re-dock. Worked example Health Warning:\n224\t\n225\t  ```\n226\t  - **Planning note (epic size)**: the <epic title> epic is ~1.5 sprints by the strategy's\n227\t    own effort estimate — above the ~2-week guideline. Kept as one logical change: a split\n228\t    would duplicate the call-site audit and fragment a single mechanical conversion.\n229\t    **Re-split trigger**: re-split this epic if the audit finds materially more call sites\n230\t    than the strategy's estimate.\n231\t  ```\n232\t\n233\t### Handling unclear strategy passages\n234\t\n235\tWhen the strategy is unclear about something, apply this two-way distinction:\n236\t\n237\t| Situation | Handling |\n238\t|-----------|---------|\n239\t| **Implementation detail** — the team will resolve this when they start the work (version choices, API surface discovery, config decisions, validation of assumptions) | Not a flag. Capture as an AC on the relevant epic if needed. Most unclear passages fall here. |\n240\t| **Genuine unknown** — nobody knows yet, resolution requires technical work, and the answer changes which downstream epics exist or what they do | Investigation epic with conditional downstream epics. |\n241\t\n242\tWhen a strategy explicitly flags something as an open question, pending review, or conditional ADR, treat it as a genuine unknown — the strategy author has already classified it. Do not downgrade to an implementation detail based on descriptive text elsewhere in the strategy. A detailed description of current state is context, not a decision. If the strategy indicates the resolution could require a different approach (conditional ADRs, \"if X requires changes to Y\"), evaluate which downstream epics build on the current assumption and gate them on the resolution.\n243\t\n244\t## Step 7: Derive Acceptance Criteria\n245\t\n246\tEach epic gets acceptance criteria derived from:\n247\t- Strategy acceptance criteria allocated to this epic's scope\n248\t- HLRs mapped to this epic\n249\t- Implementation-specific requirements (build pipeline green, rollback plan, doc review, etc.)\n250\t- Rules 23-25 from Step 5\n251\t\n252\tEvery AC must be a testable pass/fail condition on THIS epic's own deliverable — something the epic's engineering work produces or verifies. Externally-controlled preconditions are not ACs, in either form: schedule constraints (turnaround you do not control — accredited-lab queues, review-board cadence, upstream vendor timing) or availability preconditions (procurement, dedicated hardware or node allocation, partner-lab access, environment provisioning) — anything granted by parties outside the strategy belongs in the epic's Risks/Notes or the summary's Health Warnings. Write the AC at the engineering completion point you do control — \"the evidence package is submitted to the certification lab and triaged\" is a valid AC; \"lab scheduling and review-board cadence are externally controlled and cannot be compressed\" is a schedule-risk note misfiled as an AC; \"dedicated hardware nodes are available to the campaign\" is an availability precondition misfiled as an AC — the epic's work may include *securing* access, but the access being granted is not a condition the epic's deliverable produces, so it stays in Risks/Notes while the AC sits on the deliverable itself (e.g. the campaign's validated report).\n253\t\n254\t## Step 8: Generate Artifacts\n255\t\n256\t### Step 8a: Write decomposition summary (the plan)\n257\t\n258\tWrite the decomposition summary **first** — this is the blueprint for all epic files.\n259\t\n260\tWrite `artifacts/epic-tasks/{ID}-decomposition.md` in two steps:\n261\t\n262\t1. Write the body content (no frontmatter delimiters) with these sections:\n263\t   - **Epic List** (table: ID, title, type, team, priority)\n264\t   - **Dependency DAG** (Mermaid `graph TD` diagram — roots at top, arrows point from dependency to dependent: `E001 --> E003` means \"E001 must complete before E003 can start\")\n265\t   - **DAG Justification** (table: edge, rule, rationale)\n266\t   - **HLR Traceability Matrix** (HLR → epic mapping, confirming full coverage)\n267\t   - **Health Warnings** (priority inversions, scope traps — if any)\n268\t   - **Tiered Delivery** (if applicable — Tier 1 vs Tier 2 split)\n269\t\n270\t2. Set frontmatter via script:\n271\t\n272\t```bash\n273\tpython3 scripts/frontmatter.py set artifacts/epic-tasks/{ID}-decomposition.md \\\n274\t    parent_strat=\"{ID}\" epic_count=<N> critical_path_length=<N>\n275\t```\n276\t\n277\tAdd `triage=<value> triage_rationale=\"<reason>\"` for triaged strategies (Step 0).\n278\t\n279\t### Scope constraint: decompose, don't design\n280\t\n281\tYour job is to break strategy scope into units of work, not to make implementation decisions.\n282\tIf the strategy describes a capability at a functional level, the epic scope stays at that level.\n283\tDo not invent API paths, URL schemas, response formats, environment variables, caching policies,\n284\tCRD field names, or other implementation details not stated in the strategy. Those are decisions\n285\tfor the implementing team.\n286\t\n287\t### Step 8b: Write per-epic files\n288\t\n289\tWrite one file per epic to `artifacts/epic-tasks/{ID}-ENNN.md` (e.g., `{ID}-E001.md`, `{ID}-E002.md`), following the decomposition summary as the plan. Each epic's `dependencies`, `priority`, `type`, and HLR mappings must match what the summary specifies.\n290\t\n291\tFor each epic, write in two steps:\n292\t\n293\t1. Write the body content (no frontmatter delimiters) with these sections (minimum — add additional sections when the strategy contains relevant content for this epic's scope, e.g., risks, assumptions, open questions, stakeholder commitments):\n294\t   - **Title** (one line)\n295\t   - **Description** (what this epic delivers)\n296\t   - **Scope** (specific changes in this epic)\n297\t   - **Acceptance Criteria** (derived from strategy)\n298\t   - **HLR Traceability** (which strategy HLRs this epic covers)\n299\t   The four section headings are literal and required for EVERY epic, including `docs-authoring` epics — an outline of content topics goes INSIDE the `## Scope` section, never as a renamed `## Content Outline` heading (a renamed heading fails the artifact contract).\n300\t   Signal rationales go in a separate file (see Step 6) — do not include them in the epic body.\n301\t\n302\t   **No cross-references to sibling epics.** Do not reference other epics by draft ID (e.g., \"E001\", \"E003\") anywhere in the body — not in descriptions, scope, acceptance criteria, or HLR traceability. Draft IDs are internal to the decomposition and meaningless once epics are created in Jira. Dependency relationships are captured by frontmatter `dependencies` and Jira Blocks links. Each epic body must be self-contained: describe what it delivers and depends on in plain terms (e.g., \"the OTEL env var injection capability\" not \"E003\") without naming sibling epics.\n303\t\n304\t2. Set frontmatter via script:\n305\t\n306\t```bash\n307\tpython3 scripts/frontmatter.py set artifacts/epic-tasks/{ID}-E001.md \\\n308\t    epic_id=\"{ID}-E001\" title=\"<epic title>\" parent_strat=\"{ID}\" \\\n309\t    component=\"<canonical name from .context/rhai-components.txt>\" team=\"<owner team>\" \\\n310\t    type=Implementation priority=P0 \\\n311\t    dependencies=\"{ID}-E002,{ID}-E003\" \\\n312\t    ai_signals.change_specificity=1 \\\n313\t    ai_signals.pattern_precedent=1 \\\n314\t    ai_signals.adapter_pattern=0 \\\n315\t    ai_signals.existing_foundation=1 \\\n316\t    ai_signals.open_questions=-1 \\\n317\t    ai_signals.external_dependency=0 \\\n318\t    ai_signals.human_process_gates=-1 \\\n319\t    ai_signals.repo_access=1 \\\n320\t    ai_signals.architecture_claims=1\n321\t```\n322\t\n323\tFor an **Investigation** epic, set `type=Investigation` and write the five\n324\t`investigation_signals` instead of `ai_signals` (do not set both):\n325\t\n326\t```bash\n327\tpython3 scripts/frontmatter.py set artifacts/epic-tasks/{ID}-E001.md \\\n328\t    epic_id=\"{ID}-E001\" title=\"<epic title>\" parent_strat=\"{ID}\" \\\n329\t    component=\"<canonical name>\" team=\"<owner team>\" \\\n330\t    type=Investigation priority=P0 \\\n331\t    investigation_signals.question_specificity=1 \\\n332\t    investigation_signals.source_accessibility=1 \\\n333\t    investigation_signals.local_runnability=1 \\\n334\t    investigation_signals.cluster_hardware_dependence=0 \\\n335\t    investigation_signals.human_judgment_required=0\n336\t```\n337\t\n338\tAdd optional fields only when non-null: `implementation_type=<value>`, `branch=<value>`, `gated_by=<epic_id>`, `gate_failure_impact.action=<value> gate_failure_impact.fallback_approach=\"<text>\"`. Every epic that depends on an Investigation should have `gated_by` pointing to that Investigation (see Rule 3).\n339\t\n340\tDo **not** include `ai_implementability` or `ai_implementability_score` — the pipeline computes those from `ai_signals` (Implementation) or `investigation_signals` (Investigation) automatically.\n341\t\n342\t### Step 8c: Verify consistency\n343\t\n344\tAfter writing all epic files, run:\n345\t```\n346\tpython3 scripts/frontmatter.py batch-read artifacts/epic-tasks/{ID}-*E[0-9][0-9][0-9].md\n347\t```\n348\t\n349\tCompare the output against the decomposition summary. If any epic file's `dependencies`, `priority`, `type`, or HLR mappings diverged from the plan, fix the epic file to match the summary."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b00bd1bb76f169d